<!-- ==================== HALACHA 2 ==================== -->

<div class="halacha-header">
  <h2>The Laws of Rising in the Morning — Halacha 2</h2>
</div>

<div class="sa-excerpt">
  <span class="sa-label">Shulchan Aruch, Orach Chaim 1:1</span>
  "One should muster the strength of a lion to rise in the morning, that he should be," etc.
</div>

<p>Based on the Torah-teaching "Ashray HaAm — Hashgachah" <span class="source">(Likutay Moharan I, 13)</span>, see there.</p>

<p>That is, one must rise with <span class="key">alacrity</span>, without laziness. And this is the aspect of <i>emunah</i>, as our Rebbe said <span class="source">(Sefer HaMiddos, entry: <i>Zerizus</i>)</span>: that alacrity is the aspect of <i>emunah</i>. For at night, the <i>emunah</i> is among the <i>kelipos</i>, and in the morning it grows and is aroused. Therefore one must rise from his sleep with alacrity, to strengthen himself in <i>emunah</i>, in order to build and to magnify the <i>emunah</i>. And this is: <span class="verse">"that he should be the one who awakens the dawn [<i>shachar</i>]"</span> — that is, the aspect of <i>ayeles hashachar</i> [the dawn-deer], the aspect of <i>Malchus</i>, the aspect of <i>emunah</i>.</p>

<p>And this is the aspect of <span class="key">breaking the desire for money</span>. For through this, one can elevate the souls and draw down Torah, as is written in the Torah-teaching "Ashray HaAm — Hashgachah" <span class="source">(Likutay Moharan I, 13)</span> — see there. For each and every day, one must receive the Torah anew, as our Sages, of blessed memory, said <span class="source">(Sifri, <i>Vaeschanan</i>)</span>: <span class="verse">"Which I command you today"</span> <span class="source">(Deuteronomy 6:6)</span> — each and every day they should appear in your eyes as new. And the receiving of the Torah is through elevating the soul and renewing it, as explained there. And this is accomplished through breaking the desire for money, as above — which is the aspect of the awakening from sleep with alacrity, the aspect of <i>emunah</i>, as above. For the desire for money is the aspect of <i>kefiros</i> [heretical denials], the aspect of night, the aspect of darkness, <i>anpin chashuchin</i> [darkened face], the aspect of sadness and laziness, the aspect of death, the aspect of sleep — which is one-sixtieth of death <span class="source">(Berachos 57b)</span>. And one must have the strength to break the sleep, which is the aspect of death, the aspect of the desire for money. And then he can renew his soul and elevate it, to draw down Torah.</p>

<p>And then, when he breaks the sleep — the desire for money — then on the contrary, the sleep itself becomes good for him specifically. For through the sleep, he renews his soul, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"They are new every morning; great is Your faithfulness [<i>emunah</i>]"</span> <span class="source">(Lamentations 3:23)</span>. And similarly, through the money itself, he renews his soul — through conducting business with <i>emunah</i> — which is the aspect of sleep, as is written in the Torah-teaching "Ashray HaAm — Zarka" <span class="source">(Likutay Moharan I, 35)</span>. And this is the meaning of: <span class="verse">"How long, O sluggard, will you lie down? When will you rise from your sleep?"</span> <span class="source">(Proverbs 6:9)</span> — that is, how long will you lie and sleep in laziness? When will you break the sleep? For then, on the contrary, through the sleep his mind will be strengthened and his soul will be renewed, as above. And this is: <span class="verse">"When will you rise from your sleep?"</span> — that is, when will you have an elevation [<i>tekumah</i>] — that your soul will rise and be uplifted through the sleep? And when one merits to elevate his soul through breaking the sleep — through this, he effects a unification of <i>Kudsha Brich Hu</i> and the <i>Shechinah</i>. And this is: <span class="verse">"that he should be the one who awakens the dawn [<i>shachar</i>]"</span> — to arouse the <i>ayeles hashachar</i> for union.</p>

<p>And this is the meaning of: <span class="verse">"he should muster the strength of a lion [<i>ari</i>]"</span> — this is the aspect of the gathering of the souls to elevate them, as our Rebbe wrote in the Torah-teaching "Ashray HaAm" <span class="source">(Likutay Moharan I, 13)</span>, the aspect of: <span class="verse">"I have gathered [<i>arisi</i>] my myrrh with my spice"</span> <span class="source">(Song of Songs 5:1)</span>. And through this, one draws down complete <i>hashgachah</i> [Divine providence], through drawing near to the power of sight through the Torah. And this is: <span class="verse">"I have set Hashem before me always"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 16:8)</span> — that is, that His <i>hashgachah</i> is upon us always.</p>

<p>And this is what is written <span class="source">(Shulchan Aruch, Orach Chaim 1:3)</span>: that it is fitting for every G-d-fearing person to be distressed and to worry over the destruction of the <i>Beis HaMikdash</i> — for whoever mourns over Jerusalem merits to see its joy, as our Sages, of blessed memory, said <span class="source">(Bava Basra 60b)</span>. It emerges that through being distressed and worrying over the <i>Beis HaMikdash</i> — this is the aspect of its building. And the building of the <i>Beis HaMikdash</i> is the aspect of the building of <i>da'as</i> — through which one can elevate souls, as above — as explained in <i>siman</i> 13 mentioned above, see there. And this is the aspect of <i>kimas chatzos</i> [rising at midnight], when one breaks the sleep — which is the aspect of the desire for money — and mourns over Jerusalem and the destruction of the <i>Beis HaMikdash</i>, and through this, one builds it. And through the aspect of the building of the <i>Beis HaMikdash</i>, through this, one can gather the souls and elevate them. And therefore, <i>chatzos</i> sweetens judgments — for one breaks the sleep, which is the aspect of death, the aspect of <i>kefiros</i>, as above — for <span class="aramaic">"as long as there is idolatry"</span> <span class="source">(Sifri, <i>Re'eh</i>)</span> — etc. And through <i>kimas chatzos</i>, one establishes the <i>emunah</i> and draws down <i>chasadim</i>. And this is what our Rebbe said <span class="source">(Likutay Moharan I, 149)</span>: that <i>chatzos</i> is a <i>segulah</i> [propitious remedy] like a <i>pidyon</i> [redemption] — like a <i>pidyon</i> specifically. For the <i>pidyon</i> is the aspect of <i>tzedakah</i>, which breaks the desire for money and sweetens the judgments. And similarly, <i>chatzos</i> is the breaking of the sleep, which is the aspect of the desire for money, as above — through which one sweetens judgments, as above.</p>

<!-- ==================== HALACHA 3 ==================== -->

<div class="halacha-header">
  <h2>The Laws of Rising in the Morning — Halacha 3</h2>
</div>

<div class="sa-excerpt">
  <span class="sa-label">Shulchan Aruch, Orach Chaim 1:1</span>
  "One should muster the strength of a lion to rise in the morning, that he should be the one who awakens the dawn. 'I have set Hashem before me always,'" etc.
</div>

<span class="ois">§1</span>

<p>Based on the Torah-teaching "Vayaseiv Elokim" <span class="source">(Likutay Moharan I, 62)</span>, see there.</p>

<p>It is explained there that there is a distinction between types of <span class="key">difficulties [<i>kushyos</i>]</span>. For there is a <i>kushya</i> for which a person can understand a resolution. Regarding this it is stated <span class="source">(Avos 2:14)</span>: "Know what to answer," etc. For in truth, whatever Hashem <i>yisbarach</i> has bounded within the human intellect — that is, what one is capable of understanding — it is a great <i>mitzvah</i> to sharpen the mind to understand the matter thoroughly. And regarding this it is stated: "Know what to answer the <i>apikorus</i>." However, there is a <i>kushya</i> for which it is not within the capacity of human intellect to understand a resolution — only in the future will the resolution be revealed. It is forbidden for a person to delve into these, and whoever relies upon his intellect and delves into them — regarding this it is stated: <span class="verse">"All who come to her will not return"</span> <span class="source">(Proverbs 2:19)</span>. For upon such a <i>kushya</i>, it is forbidden to rely on one's intellect; rather, one must stand firmly upon <i>emunah</i>. And even regarding those <i>kushyos</i> that do have answers, sometimes the pathways of the intellect are blocked, and one does not know how to respond, and heretical thoughts are cast into him, etc. And this heresy is for each person according to his level — each one can understand, to a greater or lesser degree. For one must serve Hashem with both inclinations, etc.</p>

<p>And in truth, if a person had <span class="key">complete <i>yediah</i></span> [knowledge] — with a whole heart — that the entire world is filled with His glory, and that the Holy One, blessed be He, stands over him during prayer and hears his prayer — he would surely pray with great fervor and would be very exacting in directing his words. But because a person does not know this with a complete heart, therefore he is not so fervent and is not so exacting, etc. And all this results from the concealment of <i>yediah</i>, from the aspect of <i>machlokes</i> [strife], the aspect of <span class="verse">"their heart is divided"</span> <span class="source">(Hosea 10:2)</span> — the aspect of the <i>kushyos</i> that arise because the pathways of the intellect have been blocked, until one does not know how to answer the heresy within his heart. And the rectification for this <i>machlokes</i> within the heart is to <span class="key">study the <i>poskim</i></span> [halachic decisors]. For through this, one merits peace, etc. And through this, one will merit to nullify the <i>kushyos</i> within his heart, and merit to pray with a whole heart, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"with all your heart"</span> — <span class="aramaic">"with both your inclinations"</span> <span class="source">(Berachos 54a)</span>. And this is: <span class="verse">"I shall thank You with uprightness of heart, when I learn Your righteous judgments"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 119:7)</span> — etc. See there. However, the <i>kushyos</i> for which there is no resolution — it is forbidden to delve into them; rather, one must strengthen oneself in <i>emunah</i>.</p>

<p>And when one has <i>emunah</i> in its completeness, then through his eating, a unification of <i>Kudsha Brich Hu</i> and the <i>Shechinah</i> can be effected, etc. For the <i>birur</i> [sifting] of the foods is through <i>emunah</i>, etc. And the essential perfection and adornment of <i>emunah</i> is specifically to draw close those who are far away, etc. And for this, one must elevate the sparks of <i>dibur</i>, and then the <i>dibur</i> itself turns around and gathers the remaining sparks of holiness from the <i>kelipos</i>, etc. And to elevate the sparks of <i>dibur</i>, one needs a <i>ta'anis</i> [fast], etc. For through the desire for eating and drinking — which are the chief desires — when they overpower, G-d forbid, they draw the <i>dibur</i> into the exile of Egypt, etc. And then one must cleave to the back of the holiness, so that [the <i>kelipos</i>] will not nurse from it. And the cleaving to the back of the holiness is through the <i>ta'anis</i>, etc. Also, the essential thing in all matters is the <span class="key">beginning</span>, for all beginnings are difficult, etc. And then, when one cleaves to the back of the holiness, where it is impossible for the <i>kelipos</i> to enter and nurse — then the face of the holiness is revealed, etc. — in order to extract from their mouths what they have swallowed, etc. And then, all those who are far away turn to the faith of Israel, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"Then I will turn to the peoples a pure language"</span> <span class="source">(Zephaniah 3:9)</span> — <span class="verse">"I will turn"</span> specifically, for the pure language — the aspect of the <i>dibur</i> that is sifted from among them — turns its face toward the nations in order to gather from them the remaining sparks of holiness. And then it is fulfilled: <span class="verse">"to call, all of them, upon the Name of Hashem,"</span> etc. And this is the aspect of the adornment of <i>emunah</i>, as above. And this is <span class="source">(Zohar, <i>Saba D'Mishpatim</i>)</span>: <span class="aramaic">"The beautiful maiden who has no eyes… she emerges in the morning and is concealed in the daytime."</span> For the <i>emunah</i> is renewed for a person each morning, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"They are new every morning; great is Your faithfulness [<i>emunah</i>]"</span> <span class="source">(Lamentations 3:23)</span>. And "she is concealed in the daytime" — due to the distraction of worldly affairs, the <i>emunah</i> becomes concealed, etc. And then the <i>emunah</i> advocates on behalf of the distant ones before Hashem <i>yisbarach</i>, that He should draw them close, etc. And this is: <span class="verse">"And Yehudah approached him"</span> <span class="source">(Genesis 44:18)</span> — <span class="aramaic">"this is the approach of a king to a King"</span> <span class="source">(Zohar, <i>Vayigash</i>)</span> — etc. <span class="verse">"For you are like Pharaoh"</span> — just as Pharaoh decrees and does not fulfill, etc. — so too you decree and do not fulfill. For the <i>tzadik</i> annuls, etc. — see there all this well.</p>

<span class="ois">§2</span>

<p>And this is the aspect of: <span class="verse">"One should muster the strength of a lion to rise in the morning, that he should be the one who awakens the dawn [<i>shachar</i>]."</span> For the <i>emunah</i> is the aspect of <i>Malchus</i>, which is called <i>shachar</i>, the aspect of <i>ayeles hashachar</i>, as is known. For the essential completeness of <i>emunah</i> is in the morning, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"They are new every morning; great is Your faithfulness [<i>emunah</i>]"</span> — which is the aspect of <span class="aramaic">"she emerges in the morning [<i>nafkas b'tzafra</i>],"</span> as explained above in the Torah-teaching mentioned above. And for this reason, the <i>emunah</i> is called <i>shachar</i>, as above. And therefore, each person must muster the strength of a lion and exert himself greatly to rise in the morning, early, before daybreak — in order to engage in Torah and prayer — to elevate and complete and magnify the holy <i>emunah</i>. And this is the aspect of <span class="verse">"that he should be the one who awakens the dawn [<i>shachar</i>]"</span> — the aspect of <span class="verse">"I shall awaken the dawn"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 57:9)</span> — that is, that through his service before daybreak, he should merit to awaken the <i>shachar</i>, which is the aspect of the <i>emunah</i>, which needs to be magnified and completed before daybreak. For then is the essential time of her ascent and her completion, in the aspect of "They are new every morning," etc. — the aspect of <span class="aramaic">"she emerges in the morning."</span></p>

<p>And the essential completeness of the service is to muster great strength to rise every night at <span class="key"><i>chatzos halailah</i></span>, as explained in the Holy <i>Zohar</i> in many, many places — the great urgency that everyone in whose heart the fear of Hashem stirs is obligated to rise at <i>chatzos</i> specifically. For the essential completeness of the holy <i>emunah</i> is through nullifying, breaking, and purging from one's mind the <span class="key">two types of <i>kushyos</i> and <i>kefiros</i></span> mentioned above. That is: the <i>kushyos</i> for which it is impossible to find any answer or resolution — regarding which it is stated: <span class="verse">"All who come to her will not return"</span> <span class="source">(Proverbs 2:19)</span> — and one must rely only on <i>emunah</i> alone, as above. And further, there are <i>kushyos</i> that can be resolved, etc. — and even these <i>kushyos</i>, sometimes the pathways of the intellect are blocked and it is impossible to resolve them. And these <i>kushyos</i> are for each and every person according to his level, according to the intellect of each one. And the rectification — to merit to open the <i>da'as</i> and to reveal the pathways of the intellect — is through the study of the <i>poskim</i>, etc., as above. And then one merits peace and the <i>machlokes</i> is nullified — which are the aspect of <i>kefiros</i> and heresy — and one merits the <i>da'as</i> to answer the heresy within his heart, etc., as above.</p>

<p>And for this reason, one must muster strength to rise at <i>chatzos</i>. For day and night, which are the aspects of light and darkness, are the aspects of <i>da'as</i> and <i>kesilus</i> [foolishness] — which is the concealment of knowledge — as explained in the words of our Rebbe, of blessed memory, in the Torah-teaching "Dirshu," etc. <span class="source">(Likutay Moharan I, 37)</span>. That <i>da'as</i> is the aspect of light, and <i>kesilus</i> — the concealment of knowledge — is the aspect of darkness. For the darkness is drawn from the darkness of the <i>da'as</i> — the concealment of knowledge — from which these <i>kushyos</i> mentioned above are drawn. For it is brought in the <i>Zohar</i>: <span class="aramaic">"When the night spreads its wings, then the gates of <i>Gan Eden</i> close, and the avenging officers go out into the world, and the world is conducted by the hand of the <i>Matronisa</i>."</span> That is, at night, the darkness overpowers — which is the absence of <i>da'as</i> — which is the closing of the gates of <i>Gan Eden</i>, which are the aspects of the pathways of the intellect. And then "the avenging officers go out into the world" — which are the aspects of the judgments and <i>kelipos</i>, whose essential hold is in the <i>kefiros</i> and <i>kushyos</i> drawn through the departure of the <i>da'as</i> at night — which is the aspect of the closing of the gates of <i>Gan Eden</i>, as is known, and as above. <span class="aramaic">"And then the world is conducted by the hand of the <i>Matronisa</i>"</span> — which is the aspect of the holy Queen, which is the aspect of the holy <i>emunah</i>. For at night, at the time of darkness — the departure of <i>da'as</i> — then one must strengthen oneself only with <i>emunah</i> alone, which is the aspect of <i>Malchus</i>, the aspect of the <i>Matronisa</i> mentioned above. And this is the aspect of <span class="verse">"and Your faithfulness [<i>emunah</i>] at night"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 92:3)</span>, as brought in the words of our Rebbe, of blessed memory, in another place, see there <span class="source">(Likutay Moharan I, 35)</span>. And this is the aspect of the sleep of the night. For at night, the darkness is drawn into the world. And the essential darkness is drawn from the supernal darkness, which is the aspect of the depth of the unfathomable, the aspect of: <span class="verse">"He made darkness His hiding place"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 18:12)</span> — which are the aspect of the <i>kushyos</i> that cannot be resolved, and for which it is impossible to find any answer in this world, under any circumstance. For these <i>kushyos</i> are the aspect of darkness that conceals the revelation of His G-dliness, <i>yisbarach</i>. And this darkness cannot be dispelled through any <i>da'as</i> — only through <i>emunah</i> alone, as above. For it is impossible to find a resolution to these <i>kushyos</i>, and one must rely only on <i>emunah</i> alone, as above. And this is the aspect of the sleep of the night — for sleep is the aspect of the departure of <i>da'as</i>. And then, during sleep, the <i>da'as</i> enters into <i>emunah</i> and is renewed there, the aspect of: <span class="verse">"They are new every morning; great is Your faithfulness [<i>emunah</i>]"</span> — as explained in the words of our Rebbe, of blessed memory, in the Torah-teaching "Ashray HaAm," etc. <span class="source">(Likutay Moharan I, 35)</span> — see there. That is, during the darkness of the night — when these <i>kelipos</i> and <i>kefiros</i> and <i>kushyos</i> that cannot be resolved begin to rule over the world, which are the aspect of the darkness of night — then one needs sleep, which is a rectification and a rest for the mind. For then one must remove his <i>da'as</i> entirely — which is the aspect of sleep, the departure of <i>da'as</i> — and rely only on <i>emunah</i> alone. And this is the essential aspect of sleep: that one removes the <i>da'as</i> entirely and relies only on <i>emunah</i>, which is the aspect of the acceptance of the yoke of the Heavenly Kingdom that one accepts upon oneself before sleep, through <i>Krias Shema</i> recited upon the bed — which is the <i>emunah</i> of the unity. For during sleep, the <i>mochin</i> [mental faculties] enter into <i>emunah</i>. For against those <i>kushyos</i> that cannot be resolved — which are the aspect of the overwhelming darkness of the night — there one must remove his <i>da'as</i> entirely, in the aspect of actual sleep, and rely only on <i>emunah</i> alone.</p>

<span class="ois">§3</span>

<p>And this is the aspect of <span class="key"><i>kimas chatzos</i></span>. For even though sleep is a great rectification for the <i>mochin</i> — for they are renewed through the <i>emunah</i> during the time of sleep, as above — nevertheless, one must be careful to rise at midnight. For the night, which is the darkness — the absence of <i>da'as</i> — comprises two parts: the first half of the night and the second half of the night, as is brought. And they correspond to the two types of <i>kushyos</i> mentioned above. For in the first half of the night, the darkness and the judgment are greatest — for then the darkness is drawn from the aspect of the <i>kushyos</i> that have no answer. And these <i>kushyos</i> are the aspect of the overwhelming darkness, for it is impossible to resolve them. And therefore, then one needs sleep, which is the departure of the <i>da'as</i>. For then one must remove his <i>da'as</i> entirely and rely only on <i>emunah</i> — which is the aspect of sleep, as above. But at midnight, the judgment is sweetened, and then these <i>kushyos</i> depart from the <i>da'as</i>. And then one must rise and engage in Torah and <i>avodah</i>. And through this, one draws down the <i>da'as</i> and opens the pathways of the intellect, in order to merit complete <i>da'as</i> — to resolve and understand the answers to those <i>kushyos</i> that can be resolved, as above. And this is: <span class="verse">"At midnight I rise to give thanks to You for Your righteous judgments"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 119:62)</span> — <span class="verse">"for Your righteous judgments"</span> specifically. For rising at <i>chatzos</i> to engage in Torah in order to draw down the <i>da'as</i>, etc. — as above — this is the aspect of the study of the <i>poskim</i> mentioned above. For through this, one draws down the <i>da'as</i> to resolve those <i>kushyos</i> that can be resolved — and this one merits through engaging in Torah at <i>chatzos</i>, as above. And as is brought in the Holy <i>Zohar</i> and in the words of our Rebbe, of blessed memory <span class="source">(Likutay Moharan I, 149)</span>: that through rising at <i>chatzos</i> and through engaging in Torah then, the <i>da'as</i> is drawn down. That is, as above: at <i>chatzos</i>, in the second half of the night, the judgment is sweetened, and the darkness then is drawn only from the aspect of the <i>kushyos</i> that can be resolved — only that the pathways of the <i>da'as</i> have been blocked, which is the aspect of the darkness of night. And therefore, one must then engage in Torah in order to open the pathways of the <i>da'as</i>, in order to merit complete <i>da'as</i> by the light of day, in order to elevate and magnify the <i>emunah</i> to its completeness at the break of dawn — when she is completed, in the aspect of <span class="aramaic">"she emerges in the morning."</span> And this is the aspect of <span class="verse">"that he should be the one who awakens the dawn,"</span> as above.</p>

<span class="ois">§4</span>

<p>And this is why the <i>Shulchan Aruch</i> then juxtaposes: <span class="verse">"'I have set Hashem before me always' — this is a great principle of the Torah, for a person's sitting, his movements,"</span> etc. — as stated in the <i>Shulchan Aruch</i> <span class="source">(Orach Chaim 1:1)</span>. That is, one must know and believe that the entire earth is filled with His glory, and that he sits and stands, etc., always before the King of kings, the Holy One, blessed be He, etc. For this one merits through awakening the <i>shachar</i>, through <i>kimas chatzos</i> — through which one draws down the <i>da'as</i>, etc. — as above — until one merits a complete <i>yediah</i> that the entire earth is filled with His glory — [and this is connected] to the awakening of the <i>shachar</i>. For this depends upon that, as above. For one merits a complete <i>yediah</i>, with a whole heart, that the entire earth is filled with His glory — through the <i>da'as</i> drawn down through <i>kimas chatzos</i>, which is the aspect of the <span class="key">study of the <i>poskim</i></span>, the aspect of <span class="verse">"at midnight I rise 'to give thanks to You' for Your righteous judgments"</span> — as above. And this is <span class="verse">"to give thanks to You"</span> — the aspect of <span class="verse">"I shall thank You with uprightness of heart, when I learn Your righteous judgments"</span> — which is explained in the Torah-teaching mentioned above, and which one merits through <i>chatzos</i>, as above. And <i>kimas chatzos</i> — this is the essential aspect of the awakening of the <i>shachar</i> stated in the <i>Shulchan Aruch</i>. For the essential thing is to rise at <i>chatzos</i>. And even one who cannot rise at <i>chatzos</i>, when he musters strength at least to rise before daybreak to engage in Torah and <i>avodah</i> — through this, he opens the pathways of the intellect, etc. — according to his level and according to his service. Through this, one merits to know, with a whole heart, that the entire earth is filled with His glory — for the <i>kushyos</i> mentioned above are removed, as above. And therefore, juxtaposed to this is: <span class="verse">"I have set Hashem before me always"</span> — etc. — that is, to know that the entire earth is filled with His glory. For this depends upon that, as above. For the essential awakening of the <i>shachar</i> is for the sake of the <span class="key">rectification of prayer</span> — in order to merit to pray the morning prayer with great <i>kavanah</i>, with a whole heart. For through the awakening of the <i>shachar</i>, when one engages then in Torah and <i>avodah</i>, through this the pathways of the intellect are opened and one merits to nullify the <i>kushyos</i> and the heresy. And then one merits complete <i>da'as</i> — that the entire earth is filled with His glory — which is the aspect of <span class="verse">"I have set Hashem before me always,"</span> etc. — as above. And through knowing with a whole heart that the entire earth is filled with His glory, through this one merits to pray with great <i>kavanah</i>. For when one knows that Hashem <i>yisbarach</i> stands over him during prayer and hears and listens and hearkens to each and every word that issues from his mouth — then surely he will be greatly inflamed in his prayer and will be very exacting to direct his words, as explained above. And this <i>yediah</i>, with a whole heart, that the entire earth is filled with His glory — one merits through <i>kimas chatzos</i>, which is the aspect of the awakening of the <i>shachar</i>, as above. It emerges that <i>kimas chatzos</i> is <span class="key">propitious for prayer with <i>kavanah</i></span>. And similarly it is understood from the <i>kavanos</i> of the Ari z"l: that <i>kimas chatzos</i> is a rectification and a preparation for prayer.</p>

<span class="ois">§5</span>

<p>And therefore it is <span class="key">forbidden to eat before prayer</span>. And in the <i>Zohar</i> <span class="source">(<i>Vayakhel</i> 215b)</span> and in the <i>Kesavim</i> [writings of the Ari z"l], it is extremely stringent — that even when one rises at midnight, it is forbidden to eat or drink until after the prayer. And whoever eats or drinks then is considered like a soothsayer and a diviner, may the Merciful One save us — see there. Not like those who have now proliferated among the people, who presumptuously drink before prayer until it has become like a permitted thing in their eyes, and they hang themselves on a great tree — because they found a few <i>tzadikim</i> who were very ill and who drank before prayer because they were compelled to do so. And they liken themselves to them like a monkey before a man, and they contort themselves after them and fill their bellies with the drinking of coffee before prayer — until it is even found that some eat as well before prayer. Woe to them, woe to their souls! For it is an extremely severe prohibition, as is stated in the words of our Sages, of blessed memory <span class="source">(Berachos 10b)</span> — and especially in the Holy <i>Zohar</i> and in the <i>Kesavim</i>. And we are not to liken ourselves to great <i>tzadikim</i> who had a different spirit within them — and particularly since they were very ill. And also, most <i>tzadikim</i> did not practice this way. And <span class="key">our holy Rebbe</span>, of blessed memory, related that he never in his life drank even water before prayer — even though according to the strict law of the <i>Shulchan Aruch</i>, it is permitted to drink water — even so, he would not drink even water before prayer.</p>

<p>Let us return to our matter: that according to the above, the matter of the prohibition to eat and drink before prayer is somewhat explained. For it is explained in the Torah-teaching mentioned above that the rectification of <i>emunah</i> is through elevating the sparks of <i>dibur</i>, etc. — and this one merits through the <i>ta'anis</i>, etc. For through the desire for eating and drinking, the <i>kelipos</i> overpower — which are the aspect of the ministers of Pharaoh — and they draw the <i>dibur</i> into the aspect of the narrowness of the throat [<i>meitzar hagaron</i>], and one cannot speak any word before Hashem <i>yisbarach</i>, etc. And the rectification for this is the <i>ta'anis</i> — for through making oneself hungry, through this one draws down the waters of <i>chesed</i> to moisten the narrowness of the throat, to fulfill: <span class="verse">"Call out with the throat, do not hold back"</span> <span class="source">(Isaiah 58:1)</span> — etc. And through this, the <i>emunah</i> is rectified in its completeness, etc. — as explained there well, see there. And therefore it is forbidden to eat and drink before prayer, for one must make himself hungry before prayer — and this is the aspect of a <i>ta'anis sha'os</i> [fast of hours]: that each day one makes himself hungry and fasts before prayer. And through this, one draws an illumination from the aspect of the <i>ta'anis</i>, to cleave to the back of the holiness, in order to extract the <i>dibur</i> from the narrowness of the throat, in order to adorn the holy <i>emunah</i> that is renewed each morning, in order to merit the <i>dibur</i> of prayer in its completeness. For prayer is the aspect of <i>emunah</i>, as is known — which is completed through the rectification of the <i>dibur</i>. For the essential prayer is through <i>dibur</i> specifically, and the completeness of the <i>dibur</i> is through making oneself hungry with a <i>ta'anis</i> and breaking the desire for eating, as above. And for this reason, every Jew must fast and make himself hungry at least before prayer — when it is a great prohibition by law to eat or drink, as above. For each day, before prayer, one must make himself hungry, to draw down the aspect of the <i>ta'anis</i>, in order to rectify the <i>dibur</i> of prayer — which is the aspect of the rectification and adornment of <i>emunah</i>, etc. — as above, as explained in the Torah-teaching mentioned above, see there. Also, the <i>ta'anis</i> — which enables one to cleave to the back of the holiness — this is the aspect of the <span class="key">power of the beginning</span>: that one turns his face each time toward the beginning, to receive strength from the beginning, which is the essential thing. For all beginnings are difficult, as is well explained in the Torah-teaching mentioned above. And as is explained at its conclusion there: that for this reason, one must endeavor to begin anew each time, and the service should not become old in his eyes. For the essential power of the service is according to the beginning. And therefore, one must begin anew each time — for perhaps his beginning at first was not as it should have been; therefore he must begin now — and similarly each day and each time, see there. And therefore, each morning before prayer, one must fast and not eat before prayer — in order to extract the <i>dibur</i> from the narrowness of the throat, etc., as above — through returning to the beginning, which is the aspect of cleaving to the back of the holiness, returning each time to the first letter, as explained there, see there. And this is needed in the morning, for each and every day in the morning, one must begin to engage in the service of Hashem anew, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"They are new every morning"</span> <span class="source">(Lamentations 3:23)</span> — etc. — as if one had never begun at all. And as our Sages, of blessed memory, said <span class="source">(Sifri, <i>Vaeschanan</i>)</span>: <span class="verse">"Which I command you today"</span> <span class="source">(Deuteronomy 6:6)</span> — each and every day they should appear in your eyes as new. And similarly they expounded on the verse: <span class="verse">"Pay attention and hear, O Israel — this day you have become a people"</span> <span class="source">(Deuteronomy 27:9)</span> — etc. — that each day it should appear in your eyes as if today you entered into the covenant <span class="source">(Rashi, <i>ad loc.</i>)</span>. It emerges that each day one must begin anew. Therefore it is forbidden to eat before prayer in the morning — which is the aspect of <i>ta'anis</i>, through which one draws down the light of the power of the beginning — and through this one merits to begin anew each morning, as above.</p>

